WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.700
 So now we're gonna dig into strings a bit.

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.700
 And we've already looked at how you can pull out

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:06.060
 a single character in a string.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.020
 And now we're going to look at what we call slicing.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.700
 And that is pulling chunks of a string out.

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:13.860
 And again, we're just gonna use the square bracket operator.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:18.480
 And so S, the way I say it is sub.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.240
 S sub zero through four, that's how I read this.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.020
 S sub zero through four.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.700
 So I look at the colon as through,

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.300
 I look at the brackets as sub.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.660
 And so S sub zero through four says,

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:38.460
 start at position zero and then go up through,

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.300
 but not including four, right?

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.720
 So we don't include four.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.740
 So that's probably the hardest part of this.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:47.580
 Up to, but not including, up to, but not including.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.860
 This seems counterintuitive, kinda like starting at zero

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:53.660
 seems counterintuitive, but after a while,

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:56.060
 you'll kinda get used to it and there'll be situations

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:57.140
 where you're writing code like,

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:58.860
 oh, that's why that works better.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:00.460
 But just for now, remember it.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:03.800
 Up to, but not including, it's just kind of a little thing.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:09.820
 We'll come back to when that is useful for us.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:14.660
 Six through seven, well, that ends up being starting at six,

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:16.360
 up to, but not including seven.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:18.180
 So that's why we only get the p out.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:21.540
 Now one thing that Python is pretty nice about

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.540
 is it's not gonna give you a trace back.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:26.980
 We might expect that six through 20,

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:28.180
 well, there's no 20 characters,

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:29.940
 but it's like, ah, that's okay.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:31.800
 We'll just let you stop at the end

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.020
 and we'll start at six and go all the way to the end.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:35.980
 Oh, no trace back.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:37.340
 It's almost disappointing sometimes

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:40.740
 when Python doesn't trace back when you think, ah,

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.260
 you know, if you're so obsessed about everything,

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:44.020
 I would've traced back in that situation.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:47.540
 But hey, I guess if you're allowed, you're allowed.

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:48.560
 And so there we go.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:54.260
 Now you can eliminate or omit the first or last.

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:55.660
 If you eliminate the first,

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.120
 it assumes the beginning of string.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.540
 If you eliminate the second,

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.100
 it assumes the end of the string.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:03.540
 And why you would do this, I don't know,

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.580
 but that's from beginning to end,

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:06.580
 so it's the whole string.

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:11.260
 So whole string, eight through the end is thon,

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:15.820
 and up to, but not including two is mo, all right?

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:17.320
 So you get that.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.020
 So that's pretty simple.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:21.620
 Once you've got the rest of slicing

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:23.360
 and the rest of string indexing,

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:25.980
 the notion of eliminating the first or the last

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:27.780
 of the colon expression,

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.100
 the first or second of the colon expression,

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.980
 I think is actually pretty intuitive, pretty nice.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:36.220
 We've already been concatenating strings together.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:38.940
 We overload the plus operator,

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.100
 and there is no space added.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:45.060
 Remember when you're doing print, x comma y,

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:47.440
 this comma does turn into a space,

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:48.940
 but that's not what's happening here.

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:51.180
 There is no automatic space being added,

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:52.820
 and so we see hello in there,

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:54.700
 and it just is hello there with no space.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.420
 And so if we want, we just have to concatenate the space

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:01.140
 explicitly if we wanna put spaces into strings.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.260
 The problem is is if this,

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:04.580
 you might think it's more convenient

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:06.500
 to add a space with a concatenation,

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:07.340
 but then you have to think,

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:09.680
 well, what about if I wanna concatenate things

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.100
 and not put the space in,

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.740
 then I'd need a different operator.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:15.720
 So that's kinda why it works that way.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:20.980
 We can use in differently as a logical operator,

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:24.440
 so we're using it as an iteration structure in for loops,

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:28.500
 but we can also use it as a logical operator in if statements,

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:33.100
 so it's kinda like the double equals, or not equals,

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:35.480
 or less than or equals, or something like that.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:36.860
 It's like those guys.

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:41.860
 And it returns a true or a false is n in fruit,

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:44.800
 so that's a question, and the answer is true.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.620
 Is m in fruit?

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:48.660
 No, that's the answer to a question.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:49.740
 Is nan in fruit?

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:50.900
 Doesn't have to be single character,

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:53.720
 can be more than one character, and the answer's true.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.700
 And then you say something like if a in fruit,

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.380
 and so this is the logical value

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:01.620
 that returns a true or a false, and yes, we found it.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:03.960
 So that becomes true in this particular case,

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:06.620
 so it runs the little indented bit.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:10.500
 So n is an operator in this particular situation.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.580
 In a for loop, n means something different,

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:15.520
 and we'll use in for other things as operators,

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:18.640
 as logical operators, coming up in a bit.

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:22.680
 You can compare strings, and this has to do

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:25.100
 with the character set of your computer,

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:27.260
 the character set that Python is,

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:32.140
 but in general, it is lexicographically less than,

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:34.300
 and lexicographically greater than.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:36.940
 Uppercase and lowercase are a little weird.

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:40.500
 I think when we used the max function earlier,

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:42.280
 the way my computer was set up,

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:47.440
 uppercase was less than lowercase.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:52.440
 But in general, uppercase is less than lowercase.

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:56.020
 But in general, it's bad to assume case,

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:59.000
 but there is a deterministic way to sort strings.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:03.180
 You can have something equal to, or less than,

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:08.180
 or greater than, and all those operations work naturally.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:09.320
 The less than and greater than,

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:12.060
 you have to kind of be aware of uppercase, lowercase,

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:16.820
 things like where punctuation sorts

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:19.200
 less than or greater than letters.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:20.900
 That's kind of unpredictable,

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:24.000
 and it depends on the character set of your computer,

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.100
 and something you just play with and figure out.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.980
 If you're doing sorting stuff by first name and last name,

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:31.620
 as long as the case is kind of the same,

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:39.900
 if you were sorting chuck with an uppercase and glen,

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:43.380
 the fact that these uppercases, they'd sort right,

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:45.140
 and these lowercases would sort right,

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:49.340
 but if you were to subdue instead lowercase chuck

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:54.060
 and uppercase glen, then that would sort weird.

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:56.740
 As a matter of fact, the G would come before that,

00:05:56.740 --> 00:06:00.460
 and so case can mess this up, but in general,

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:04.140
 other than case and special characters and other things,

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:06.100
 it technically works.

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:08.000
 It's just hard to kind of predict it.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.940
 A lot of what we do is use the string library,

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:14.980
 and so the strings are objects,

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:17.620
 and we'll talk later about what that really means,

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:20.820
 and objects have these things we call methods.

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:26.960
 So a string object has some built-in capabilities,

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:29.520
 and one of the built-in capabilities

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:33.260
 that the string object has is here is a string object,

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:35.220
 and because greet is a string object,

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:37.940
 if we said type, we'd see that it was an str,

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:41.140
 .lower says, hey, dear string,

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:43.540
 make a lowercase version of yourself.

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:44.900
 It's like calling this function lower

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:47.240
 and passing greet into it,

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:48.740
 and then give that back to me.

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:50.500
 Now it doesn't actually change greet,

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:52.300
 it gives me a lowercase copy.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:55.100
 So here I have hello Bob with an H and a B uppercase,

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:58.060
 and what I get back in zap is hello Bob all lowercase,

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:00.980
 and note that greet is unchanged,

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:02.920
 so hello Bob is still there,

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:05.480
 and you can even call these methods on constants,

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:09.460
 so this is a string object, quote, hi, there, quote, .lower,

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:13.740
 that says call lower on this bit of string

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.460
 and give me back a lowercase version of it,

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:19.180
 and so it prints out as the residual return value,

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:20.260
 this is like a function call,

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:22.660
 but a method call is a kind of special form

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:23.980
 of a function call.

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:26.620
 It's a function call where you say the thing dot

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:29.100
 the function name rather than function name

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:30.820
 passed in as a parameter.

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:34.260
 Like len, for example, is non-object-oriented,

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.140
 you know, len of x, that's non-object-oriented,

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:40.580
 object-oriented would be x dot something, parentheses.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:46.300
 But, so constants are objects as well,

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:49.340
 and taking the lower gives us back lowercase, hi, there,

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:51.420
 and so that's just one of the things

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:53.060
 that you can do in the string library,

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:57.140
 these are built into string variables and constants,

00:07:57.140 --> 00:07:58.300
 they're just always there.

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:01.460
 As soon as you make a string, they're part of it,

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:04.420
 and when you do type and it says it's class str,

00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:07.420
 we'll get to object-oriented, don't worry,

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:09.340
 we'll get to object-oriented.

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:12.160
 Okay, and so you can do things like use the type,

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:16.500
 if you're just, this used to say type str,

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:18.860
 but it's class str, kind of this is more of an,

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:21.660
 oh, the word class is an object-oriented concept,

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:23.780
 but it is a string, and you can use the dir,

00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:25.460
 and of course there's extra stuff up here,

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:28.420
 and this is showing all the different methods

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:33.620
 or capabilities, things we can do to strings.

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:37.980
 So, you know, x dot something, parentheses,

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:39.380
 well, what can we do there?

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:42.680
 This is all of those things that we can do to x's

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:45.560
 that are built in and come with x's,

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:49.000
 I mean, come with strings when we build them.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:52.500
 And Python, of course, has great documentation online

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:54.660
 for all of these string methods and what they do

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:57.540
 and how they work and why they work the way they do.

00:08:57.540 --> 00:08:59.900
 And so here's some of that Python documentation.

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:02.980
 We'll look at a few of these, but, you know,

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:05.780
 don't hesitate to say, Python string uppercase,

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:09.360
 and then we're like, oh yeah, yeah, that is upper, right?

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:11.740
 And so here's a few things that we can

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:16.980
 do and use, some of the ones I use a lot,

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:19.080
 and we'll look at each one of these things.

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:23.900
 So the find operation says,

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:27.980
 find me a substring within a string, right?

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:29.500
 Find me a substring within a string,

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:33.240
 so find me the first na and give me back the position,

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:35.140
 so that gives me back two.

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:39.060
 And then I can say, go find a z in there.

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:41.960
 Well, there's no z, and so it returns me negative one.

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:43.100
 So that's what the find does.

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:47.100
 So we're gonna use this kind of stuff a lot,

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:49.500
 and we do a lot of looking in strings.

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:51.260
 Converting things to upper or lowercase,

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:54.300
 there is an upper method and a lower method.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:59.180
 So greet, greet.upper, and that means the uppercase nnn

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:02.700
 is hello Bob, greet.lower, that means that www

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:06.180
 is the lowercase hello world, and greet is unchanged.

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:08.940
 Greet is still hello Bob with upper and lower,

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:11.540
 because each of these methods basically say,

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:14.840
 I'm going to give you back a uppercase copy

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:17.100
 or a lowercase copy of the original thing

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.300
 without changing the original thing.

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:26.760
 Search and replace is super useful, super duper useful,

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:28.780
 and it's pretty clean.

00:10:28.780 --> 00:10:31.340
 Here we have a string, and we use the replace method.

00:10:31.340 --> 00:10:34.740
 In this case, we're passing in the old and the new.

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:36.960
 Bob, replace all Bobs with Janes,

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:39.220
 and so that takes this hello Bob

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:41.280
 and turns it to hello Jane.

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:43.200
 Again, greet is unchanged.

