 This says go find all the O's

00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:56.540
 and replace all the O's with X's,

00:10:56.540 --> 00:10:58.500
 and so it goes and finds two of them,

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:01.020
 and then out come two X's.

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:03.340
 And so that really replaces,

00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:06.780
 not just replace the first one, but replace all of them.

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:10.620
 White space, as we'll see, is a big deal.

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:14.300
 And white space is not just blanks,

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:15.560
 although the most common thing,

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:17.460
 but it's also sort of non-printing characters

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:20.140
 like tabs and new lines and other kinds of things.

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:22.420
 And so we have a number of different ways

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:23.860
 to strip white space.

00:11:23.860 --> 00:11:26.700
 So here we've got some spaces at the beginning

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:28.180
 and spaces at the end.

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:30.300
 And we print out, we do an L strip,

00:11:30.300 --> 00:11:32.080
 and that throws away the spaces at the beginning.

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:34.180
 That's the left, so that's the left strip.

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:36.460
 It all takes any, if there's nothing there,

00:11:36.460 --> 00:11:37.900
 it doesn't harm it.

00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:40.980
 R strip means throw away all the blanks on the far end.

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:44.860
 And then strip says go take both sides,

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:46.220
 both sides for strip,

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:49.140
 and so that pulls out all the spaces on both sides.

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:50.140
 This will be useful,

00:11:50.140 --> 00:11:51.980
 'cause sometimes when you're tearing stuff apart,

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:55.020
 you'll find yourself getting extra spaces,

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:57.140
 sometimes at the beginning, sometimes at the end.

00:11:57.140 --> 00:12:01.420
 And it can be tab or new line.

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:05.900
 It's sort of white space,

00:12:05.900 --> 00:12:10.280
 space that is kind of not visible, clear.

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:11.580
 That's what white space is.

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:13.580
 It's like if you were on a piece of paper,

00:12:13.580 --> 00:12:15.060
 it's the white space.

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:16.860
 It's like X, well that's not white space,

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:18.920
 but right here, oh that's white space.

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:23.780
 It's any character that doesn't cause printing to happen,

00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:25.860
 if that makes any sense.

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:28.020
 It's any character where nothing would be printed.

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:29.460
 And there are characters like that.

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:31.500
 There's like even bell characters,

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:33.100
 but we don't use them very much.

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:35.340
 We can ask very conveniently,

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:39.640
 we can say hey, does this line start with a particular string?

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:43.500
 And so, line, this is a question,

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:45.160
 gonna return a true or a false.

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:47.660
 Does this line start with please?

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:49.980
 And the answer is true, it does start with please.

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:52.180
 Does this line start with a lowercase p?

