 colon, some block of text, some block of code.

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:06.180
 So, we can combine these things to tear stuff out.

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:09.620
 And so let's assume that what we wanna do in this case

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:12.140
 is we wanna take a from line.

00:13:12.140 --> 00:13:15.980
 This is from an email form, email format from a mailbox.

00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:20.100
 And this has got the from with a space,

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:22.580
 and the person's email, and then at sign

00:13:22.580 --> 00:13:25.140
 in the school they're from, and a space,

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:27.620
 and then the rest of the stuff like when this mail was sent.

00:13:27.620 --> 00:13:30.500
 And this is a real mail message from this guy, Stephen,

00:13:30.500 --> 00:13:32.780
 from the University of Cape Town in South Africa.

00:13:32.780 --> 00:13:35.500
 And it's really Stephen, and this really is the first line

00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:37.280
 of a file that you'll get to know pretty well

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:38.460
 by the rest of this course.

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:41.380
 Hi, Stephen, you, we like you.

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:42.940
 You are the example in my class

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:45.260
 and have been for a long time.

00:13:45.260 --> 00:13:47.340
 People actually who know Stephen have taken this class

00:13:47.340 --> 00:13:49.680
 and they're like, Stephen, I saw your picture in the class.

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:52.100
 So if you're ever in Cape Town at the University of Cape Town,

00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:54.240
 say hi to Stephen and tell him that you saw him in the class.

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:56.540
 But okay, that's neither here nor there.

00:13:56.540 --> 00:14:00.380
 What I really wanna do is I wanna extract his school

00:14:00.380 --> 00:14:03.220
 from this email line.

00:14:03.220 --> 00:14:06.640
 Okay, so, now eventually we will do things

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:08.340
 like the data will come from files,

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:09.660
 but this is still chapter six.

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:11.580
 So this is the data we're gonna search through.

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:16.380
 And so we can say, hey, let's go find the at sign.

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:18.580
 Search up to this position and find the at sign.

00:14:18.580 --> 00:14:20.660
 So data dot find at sign.

00:14:20.660 --> 00:14:22.180
 And give me back where that's at.

00:14:22.180 --> 00:14:24.460
 That's in position 21.

00:14:24.460 --> 00:14:26.020
 It's position zero.

00:14:26.020 --> 00:14:28.720
 Then what we're gonna do is we're gonna look

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:31.300
 for the next space after the at sign.

00:14:31.300 --> 00:14:33.420
 So we're gonna start at the at sign

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:35.940
 and tell find to start here and look forward

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:37.780
 until it finds a space.

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:40.060
 So data dot find, look for a space

00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:42.520
 starting at the position of the at sign.

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:45.300
 And then that'll be in position 31.

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:47.780
 So 31 is what we get in the space position.

00:14:47.780 --> 00:14:51.360
 So now what we have is we have in two variables,

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:54.860
 we have position, the position of the at sign

00:14:54.860 --> 00:14:56.740
 and the position of the space after the at sign.

00:14:56.740 --> 00:14:59.520
 Now what we really want is this bit right here.

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:02.260
 So we have to go one beyond the at sign

00:15:02.260 --> 00:15:04.580
 and we don't want the space.

00:15:04.580 --> 00:15:06.900
 So we say, we're gonna use slicing here.

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:10.640
 The data sub at position plus one up to

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:12.380
 but not including the space.

00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:14.740
 Oh, smiley face, 'cause we didn't have to say

00:15:14.740 --> 00:15:18.080
 space minus one, because that is up to

00:15:18.080 --> 00:15:21.140
 but not including.

00:15:21.140 --> 00:15:25.020
 And so we get that little bit right there.

00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:27.940
 So we don't have to say minus one there,

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:29.840
 because this is not actually included.

00:15:29.840 --> 00:15:32.340
 The thing that's at the position, the space is not included.

00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:34.060
 So that's already a little benefit

00:15:34.060 --> 00:15:36.080
 for the up to but not including.

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:38.320
 And so when we print this variable out host,

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:42.660
 we get exactly just the school that Stephen works at

00:15:42.660 --> 00:15:46.140
 and probably went to, as a matter of fact.

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:47.780
 I don't know if he went there or not.

00:15:47.780 --> 00:15:50.200
 So this is just kind of a note

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:55.180
 for non-Latin character sets.

00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:57.600
 You know, all programming languages from the '60s on

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:01.980
 tended to work in what we call the Latin character set,

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:05.520
 which is the United States and England and Europe

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:08.820
 and lots of places use this ABC character set

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:10.680
 and the special characters.

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:15.520
 But it's really common to want to use different characters.

00:16:15.520 --> 00:16:18.680
 And so if you're going from Python 2 to Python 3,

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:20.220
 and we'll talk about this a little later

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:22.600
 when it matters more.

00:16:22.600 --> 00:16:26.780
 Luckily we're in Python 3, and so one of the big things

00:16:26.780 --> 00:16:31.120
 about Python 3 is that all the internal strings are Unicode.

00:16:31.120 --> 00:16:35.420
 In Python 2, there was sort of some confusion

00:16:35.420 --> 00:16:36.940
 as you went between strings.

00:16:36.940 --> 00:16:38.520
 And this is just a little bit of code.

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:40.940
 And so I'm putting a in here.

00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:46.100
 You know, some Asian characters, this is Korean actually,

00:16:46.100 --> 00:16:48.840
 Asian characters into X, and then I say

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:52.980
 what kind of a thing this is, and that is a string.

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:55.040
 And then there's this Unicode.

00:16:55.040 --> 00:16:57.280
 And this comes from Python 2.

00:16:57.280 --> 00:17:00.960
 If it's a Unicode operation, it's still a string.

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:04.540
 Whereas in Python 2, if you put international characters

00:17:04.540 --> 00:17:06.580
 into X, then it was a string.

00:17:06.580 --> 00:17:08.980
 And then there was a separate kind of a constant

00:17:08.980 --> 00:17:11.780
 called a Unicode constant, and it was a different type.

00:17:11.780 --> 00:17:15.060
 And there was ways that you had to mess

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:17.720
 with these Unicode variables as you did things

00:17:17.720 --> 00:17:20.820
 like read them from files and put them back into files

00:17:20.820 --> 00:17:21.860
 and did other things.

00:17:21.860 --> 00:17:24.360
 So it was much more difficult

00:17:24.360 --> 00:17:29.620
 in Python 2, but we're doing in Python 3.

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:33.660
 And in Python 3, it natively understands

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:37.340
 non-Latin character sets, international Asian character sets,

00:17:37.340 --> 00:17:39.180
 Spanish, French character sets.

00:17:39.180 --> 00:17:41.460
 And so this is a good thing for Python 3,

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:44.700
 and this is one of the real benefits of using Python 3.

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:47.060
 And as we start doing stuff where we're exchanging data

00:17:47.060 --> 00:17:50.380
 with the outside world, this will come into play,

00:17:50.380 --> 00:17:53.080
 and I'll have to show you how to use it.

00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:54.500
 There was weird things that you had to do,

00:17:54.500 --> 00:17:58.100
 it just makes a lot more sense in Python 3.

00:17:58.100 --> 00:18:00.520
 So we've talked about strings.

00:18:00.520 --> 00:18:03.400
 We learned about the string, we're converting it.

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:04.540
 We've done a whole bunch of stuff.

00:18:04.540 --> 00:18:08.700
 And this is, again, we're not yet doing anything

00:18:08.700 --> 00:18:11.660
 super useful, we're learning sort of how to slice and dice

00:18:11.660 --> 00:18:13.900
 even though we're sort of not making the meal yet.

00:18:13.900 --> 00:18:16.420
 Up next, we're gonna talk about files,

00:18:16.420 --> 00:18:18.500
 we're gonna read some data, and we're gonna slice and dice

00:18:18.500 --> 00:18:21.700
 and use all the things in the next chapter

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:23.380
 that we've learned up to this point.

00:18:23.380 --> 00:18:25.380
 So see you in a bit.

